## Proof

Let $E$ be a normed affine space of finite dimension $n$ with frame $(a_0,(u_1,\dots,u_n))$, $F$ a normed affine space, $A\subseteq E$ open, and $f:A\to F$. Denote by $\vec E,\vec F$ the associated vector spaces and by $\mathcal L(\vec E;\vec F)$ the space of continuous linear maps $\vec E\to\vec F$ (all linear maps are continuous since $\vec E$ is finite-dimensional).

**Claim.** The derivative $\mathrm Df:A\to\mathcal L(\vec E;\vec F)$ is defined and continuous on $A$ if and only if, for every $i\in\{1,\dots,n\}$, the partial derivative

$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}:A\to F,\qquad \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)=\lim_{t\to 0}\frac{f(a+t\,u_i)-f(a)}{t}$$

exists at every point of $A$ and is continuous on $A$.

---

### Necessity ($\Rightarrow$)

Assume $\mathrm Df$ is defined and continuous on $A$. Fix $i$. For $a\in A$, since $A$ is open and $t\mapsto a+t\,u_i$ is continuous with value $a$ at $t=0$, there is $\varepsilon>0$ with $a+t\,u_i\in A$ for $|t|<\varepsilon$. The directional derivative of $f$ at $a$ along $u_i$ equals $\mathrm Df(a)\cdot u_i$:

$$\frac{f(a+t\,u_i)-f(a)}{t}\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}\mathrm Df(a)(u_i).$$

Hence $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)$ exists and equals $\mathrm Df(a)(u_i)$. The map $a\mapsto\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)=\mathrm Df(a)(u_i)$ is the composition of $\mathrm Df:A\to\mathcal L(\vec E;\vec F)$ (continuous by hypothesis) with the evaluation map $\ell\mapsto\ell(u_i)$, which is continuous linear on $\mathcal L(\vec E;\vec F)$. Therefore each $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}$ is continuous on $A$.

---

### Sufficiency ($\Leftarrow$)

Assume every partial derivative $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}$ exists on $A$ and is continuous on $A$. We prove $f$ is differentiable at every $a\in A$ with continuous derivative.

Fix $a\in A$ and a small displacement $h=\sum_{i=1}^n h_i\,u_i\in\vec E$. Since $A$ is open, for $\|h\|$ small enough the segment from $a$ to $a+h$ lies in $A$, and so do all intermediate points $a+h_1 u_1+\dots+h_i u_i$ used below. Define the telescoping decomposition

$$f(a+h)-f(a)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Big[f\big(a+\sum_{j=1}^{i}h_j u_j\big)-f\big(a+\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}h_j u_j\big)\Big],$$

with the convention $\sum_{j=1}^{0}=0$. Each summand is a difference of $f$ along the $u_i$-direction. Apply the one-dimensional mean value inequality to the map $t\mapsto f\big(a+\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}h_j u_j+t\,u_i\big)$ on $[0,h_i]$: its derivative in $t$ is $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}\big(a+\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}h_j u_j+t\,u_i\big)$, which exists by hypothesis. Thus

$$\Big\|f\big(a+\sum_{j=1}^{i}h_j u_j\big)-f\big(a+\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}h_j u_j\big)-h_i\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)\Big\|\;\le\;|h_i|\,\sup_{0\le t\le 1}\Big\|\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}\big(a+\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}h_j u_j+t\,h_i u_i\big)-\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)\Big\|.$$

Summing over $i$ and setting $L_a(h):=\sum_{i=1}^n h_i\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)$, which is a continuous linear map $\vec E\to\vec F$ (continuity follows from finite-dimensionality of $\vec E$), we obtain

$$\|f(a+h)-f(a)-L_a(h)\|\;\le\;\Big(\sum_{i=1}^n |h_i|\Big)\,\omega_a(h),$$

where $\omega_a(h):=\max_i\sup_{0\le t\le 1}\big\|\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(\cdot)-\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)\big\|$ over the segment from $a$ to $a+h$. By continuity of each $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}$ at $a$, $\omega_a(h)\to 0$ as $h\to 0$. Since all norms on $\vec E$ are equivalent (finite dimension), $\sum_i|h_i|\le C\|h\|$, so

$$\frac{\|f(a+h)-f(a)-L_a(h)\|}{\|h\|}\le C\,\omega_a(h)\xrightarrow[h\to 0]{}0.$$

Therefore $f$ is differentiable at $a$ with $\mathrm Df(a)=L_a=\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)\otimes u_i^*$, i.e. $\mathrm Df(a)(h)=\sum_i h_i\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)$.

It remains to show $\mathrm Df:A\to\mathcal L(\vec E;\vec F)$ is continuous. The map $a\mapsto\mathrm Df(a)$ is determined by the $n$ coordinate maps $a\mapsto\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)$ via $\mathrm Df(a)(u_i)=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(a)$. The correspondence $\mathcal L(\vec E;\vec F)\cong F^n$ given by $L\mapsto(L(u_1),\dots,L(u_n))$ is a linear homeomorphism (both spaces finite-dimensional in the domain). Under this identification, $\mathrm Df$ corresponds to $\big(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_1},\dots,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_n}\big):A\to F^n$, which is continuous because each component $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}$ is continuous by hypothesis. Hence $\mathrm Df$ is continuous on $A$.

---

### Conclusion

The necessary and sufficient condition is:

> **All $n$ partial derivatives $\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_1},\dots,\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_n}$ exist on $A$ and are continuous on $A$.**

Equivalently, $f$ is of class $C^1$ on $A$ if and only if its components in the frame $(u_1,\dots,u_n)$ admit continuous partial derivatives.

